7] [17592186116076 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116077 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116077 #edb/ref 1061 1486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116077 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33986316-e319-566e-9931-5531866da50b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116077 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116078 #edb/ref 1061 1488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116078 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8300db68-e37d-5905-a6e3-75198133f507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116078 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116087 #edb/ref 1061 15049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd0d0141-6460-5be8-8511-503ece684172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1061 30951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1b69042-4e4f-5d93-b2e6-d2792a279297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1061 30952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "029cc3c0-aac1-5ab3-b3dc-fe7cf275acc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1061 30953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0681c605-1849-50ee-9d50-5be4f585dc4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1061 30954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a44f609d-29bb-5f64-b769-1c94ec6780fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1061 34580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41a4f348-1e37-5763-9563-f9ba63ffa00a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116266 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1061 34581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88da9ae9-0589-5f70-ab03-1c92544b026c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116267 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116274 #edb/ref 1061 39428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116274 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9f0c982-62ce-5f5d-9d4d-30e10da26092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116274 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the equation $3^{3x} = 81^{x-1},$ which has two exponential terms. How do we solve it?\n\nTo solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using the fact that $81 = {\\color{red}3}^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^4\\right)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x} & ={3}^{4(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =4(x-1) \\\\ 3x & =4x-4 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhenever we can rewrite an equation so that both sides have a common base, we can solve the equation by equating the exponents." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1065 2798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91b0d933-a432-59bd-b187-7dd052926b12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose the random variable $X\\sim B(n,p)$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\nThere are certain situations where working with binomial random variables becomes difficult. For example, when the parameter $n$ is large, computing exact probabilities can become problematic due to the large numbers involved.\n\nUnder certain conditions, we can approximate the *discrete* random variable $X$ using a *continuous normal* random variable $Y,$ given by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are calculated using the formulas for the mean and variance of the binomial random variable $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mu = np, \\qquad \\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTo determine whether a given binomial distribution can be approximated using a normal distribution, we use the following rule of thumb:\n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nTo get a feel for how this works, let's consider a few examples of binomial distributions and their corresponding normal approximations:\n\n- First, consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.2.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{1}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{0.8}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = 1 < 5,\\qquad n(1-p) = 4 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{0.8}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.2).$ \n\nThe diagram below compares the two distributions. The yellow rectangles correspond to the values of the PMF of $B(5, 0.2),$ and the smooth blue curve is the PDF of the corresponding normal distribution.\n\n![](images/6a/6a67b3ad65bfedcd25b787ff15e72c575da1215ae47502ff11f97d40de407560.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{2.5}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{1.25}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 2.5 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np = n(1-p) < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{2.5}},{\\color{red}{1.25}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/0a/0a8f2d9ccd78ee885ea3bb8f2f00705f69c520f8ec0588411865399b6ecef046.png)\n\n- Finally, we consider the case where $n=20$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{10}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{5}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 10 > 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{10}},{\\color{red}{5}})$ *is* a good approximation to $B(20, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/9e/9efc7df7c423ed2d8d6cc70ef820de3cb235f644567a3fec0d0a0e501042e305.png)\n\nThe fact that we can approximate binomial random variables as normal when $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ is a consequence of the so-called **central limit theorem** (or CLT). We'll discuss this in more detail at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1065 4970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d16ee6a-2c83-55f2-a37f-2b5264587984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116536 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6).$ Let's estimate the value of $P(X=33)$ using the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12).\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =P(\\frac{32.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{33.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.72\\le Z\\le 1.01) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z<0.72) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z\\le 0.72) \\\\  & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72) \\\\  & =0.8438-0.7642 \\\\  & =0.0796.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1065 5618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "22b0d565-b5ca-5c37-9d8a-f8a2c8f83fce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116542 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the Normal Approximation to the Binomial Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1064 "In the last example, we approximated the binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim B(50,0.6)\n$$\n\nusing a normal random variable\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12),\n$$\n\nand we arrived at the approximation\n\n$$\nP(X=33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796.\n$$\n\nIf you were to use a software package to evaluate this approximation, you might get an answer like\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0791.\n$$\n\nNotice that there is a difference of around $0.0005$ between the two answers.\n\nThere is a reason for this discrepancy. When we calculated that $P(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796,$ we rounded our $z$-values two decimal places and then used a lookup table to find $\\Phi(z)$ for those rounded values. As a result, we introduced some small rounding errors. \n\nTypically, the rounding errors are small, so we will continue to work with a table of values. However, it's worth bearing this in mind if you're using a software package or calculator to compute $\\Phi(z).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1065 5619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d460cc56-a34f-5495-874f-19d80c5ad3b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116543 #edb/ref 1067 "A Note on Rounding Errors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1064 "According to the so-called **central limit theorem**, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) random variables, then\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is \"sufficiently large,\" and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X\\sim B(n,p),$ then $X$ can be thought of as a sum of $n$ I.I.D Bernoulli random variables \n\n$$\nX_i\\sim\\textrm{Bernoulli}(p),\n$$\n\nwhere the PMF of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n![](images/13/134fcb82e69ef8d5fc1e63353f3b28c036bda08176918bcd166ebc52826854c6.svg)\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2 = p(1-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting our values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ into our normal approximation for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\left(np, np(1-p)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe \"sufficiently large\" condition on $n$ is taken care of by stipulating that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- When $p\\approx 0.5,$ then $n\\approx 10$ is sufficiently large for the normal approximation to be applied.\n\n- However, if $p$ is closer to zero or one, then the distribution of $X$ is not symmetric, and consequently, we need $n$ to be much larger for the central limit theorem to be valid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1065 6514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9a338343-d2c2-54a6-98b5-5033bebeb652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116546 #edb/ref 1067 "The Normal Approximation as a Consequence of the Central Limit Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116550 #edb/ref 1064 "The properties of indefinite integrals also apply to definite integrals. For example, to evaluate the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{2}^{3} 4 x \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwe can use the constant multiple rule and factor the constant out of the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}4x\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(9-4) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, the constant multiple rule holds for any constant $k$ and any definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}kf(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =k{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116550 #edb/ref 1065 771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116550 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bdf78ae3-dc9e-5db4-a6a4-ac8883d6ab7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116550 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116551 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also use the sum rule to compute the definite integral of the sum of two functions. For example, to evaluate the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{1}^{2} (x+x^2) \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwe can split up the integral into a sum of two integrals and evaluate each integral separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{2}+\\frac{8-1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(f(x)+g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116551 #edb/ref 1065 773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116551 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4a0dd6d3-abec-56e8-a3b1-a812dfb45c5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116551 #edb/ref 1067 "Integrating the Sum of Two Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116552 #edb/ref 1064 "To compute the integral of the sum of two functions multiplied by constants, we can combine the addition rule and the constant multiple rule. For example, to compute the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^1 (3x^2 + 6x)\\,\\textrm d x ,\n$$\n\n we can split up the sum and factor out the constants, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute the two resulting integrals using the usual methods.\n\nIn general, for functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and constants $c$ and $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(cf(x)+kg(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+k{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116552 #edb/ref 1065 775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116552 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "244b2afb-58eb-526d-ba0c-7ff36d5b834f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116552 #edb/ref 1067 "Combining the Constant Multiple and Sum Rules" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117659 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117659 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117659 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117659 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117659 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117664 #edb/ref 175